00:07:00.060 --> 00:07:04.400
He turned all those other compu mines off except for Keiko's.

00:07:04.400 --> 00:07:08.480
And that's where like the game picks up is like all that shit's already happened.

00:07:08.620 --> 00:07:16.840
And now it's your job to go to these planets, find these compu mines, turn them back on and take down Agathos.

00:07:16.840 --> 00:07:19.320
And it's like, geez, that's a lot.

00:07:19.660 --> 00:07:20.880
How are we going to do it?

00:07:20.880 --> 00:07:32.180
Yeah. And, but before we get into the gameplay and the loop and everything, I wanted to talk about beyond the character model of rad gravity.

00:07:33.740 --> 00:07:48.220
This is a, this is a Western made NES game, uh, with big ideas, you know, the planet hopping, um, the, uh, like the, the concept of playing with gravity, the hub ship where you put in the coordinates.

00:07:48.220 --> 00:07:53.220
But this doesn't look like an NES game.

00:07:53.540 --> 00:08:00.280
And we've seen this with other, you know, like Western developed NES games, a boy in his blob, maniac mansion.

00:08:00.280 --> 00:08:08.920
I think those ones kind of adhered more to the NES look, but definitely like the idea of feeling a little different than how normal NES games play.

00:08:09.560 --> 00:08:11.040
Rad gravity is doing both.

00:08:11.220 --> 00:08:14.540
It doesn't quite feel like how we're used to NES games playing.

00:08:14.540 --> 00:08:18.480
And then at the same time, it doesn't quite look like other NES games.

00:08:18.920 --> 00:08:20.280
No. Are you tracking?

00:08:20.280 --> 00:08:22.260
I do track this.

00:08:22.540 --> 00:08:37.420
And, and one thing that it's a little, it's a little tangential, but I just wanted to point out that the designer of this game is the same designer as wasteland, like from like interplays very early days.

00:08:37.600 --> 00:08:39.300
That's insane. Yeah.

00:08:39.680 --> 00:08:45.000
And, and I think like, once you sort of square that circle, it makes a bit more sense.

00:08:45.000 --> 00:08:53.600
Like this game doesn't really pay, pay attention to a lot of the, the rules and norms that we've seen.

00:08:53.880 --> 00:08:57.860
Um, there's a lot of like sub objectives happening while you're playing the game.

00:08:58.100 --> 00:09:03.140
Uh, you, there's so much text, like there's text boxes that just pop up.

00:09:03.420 --> 00:09:10.660
There's actual like set piece, like story driven moments that are happening in gameplay.

00:09:10.660 --> 00:09:24.200
Like they're trying to make a much more, at least in my opinion, like modern design here with pretty like primitive tech, like technical work.

00:09:24.480 --> 00:09:27.180
I don't know if that's sort of how you're seeing it.

00:09:27.340 --> 00:09:28.840
Yeah. I think that is how I'm seeing it.

00:09:28.840 --> 00:09:41.420
And I think you kind of are blown away at first by being in that spaceship and even just like the higher fidelity of the graphics there than eventually like the, the, the bare bones game you get put into.

00:09:41.620 --> 00:09:47.080
But the, uh, like what's possible at first seems limitless.

00:09:47.080 --> 00:09:52.640
Like you're in this ship and there's other planets and like, you can, you can, you can like select from them on the map.

00:09:52.740 --> 00:09:56.060
It's not like, Oh, maybe when they'll be like, you could kind of see them, but you can't go there yet.

00:09:56.060 --> 00:09:57.100
Cause you don't know the coordinates.

00:09:57.740 --> 00:10:00.600
Um, it feels like the game is very open.

00:10:00.920 --> 00:10:04.280
And then you kind of figure out like, Oh, I'm, I'm locked into this.

00:10:04.320 --> 00:10:05.540
Actually, this is pretty small.

00:10:05.820 --> 00:10:17.380
Actually, this is just like kind of a stage by stage platforming game, but they figured out like a, a new way to get me between the levels rather than just like reach the end of the stage.

00:10:17.380 --> 00:10:29.100
I, I feel like the first, the biggest problem with the game right away is, is again, a design thing because it doesn't happen always.

00:10:29.100 --> 00:10:34.660
And especially not like once you get into like, as you go further into the game.

00:10:34.660 --> 00:10:44.680
But when I first got to the first planet and landed, and then I'm like dropped in on their world and everything, the enemies are completely inconsequential.

00:10:45.000 --> 00:10:47.840
You, there's no reason to attack them.

00:10:47.980 --> 00:10:51.240
You're better off like going around them and being like, sorry, I'm here.

00:10:51.380 --> 00:10:53.660
I know you don't like me, but I don't like you.

00:10:53.800 --> 00:10:55.100
And let's just leave each other alone.

00:10:55.640 --> 00:10:57.700
And then you're like trying to get around them.

00:10:57.940 --> 00:11:00.480
And it's like, huh, I didn't land on anything.

00:11:00.480 --> 00:11:04.060
I could have sworn those were all platforms or, huh, this isn't a wall.

00:11:04.220 --> 00:11:05.420
Like I thought this was a wall.

00:11:05.520 --> 00:11:07.900
And then all of a sudden, wait, this is a platform.

00:11:08.260 --> 00:11:10.720
Like this block looks the same as the other block.

00:11:11.720 --> 00:11:19.460
It's funny because I feel like I know what they were going for, at least with like the enemy, like the philosophy behind these enemies.

00:11:19.460 --> 00:11:28.100
Like they don't act like a lot of the other side scrolling platformer enemies that we're used to where they just have a loop.

00:11:28.240 --> 00:11:37.880
They don't really care that you're there until you actually like, like if it were like Castlevania, they would actually have like attack patterns.

00:11:38.480 --> 00:11:44.240
But in like, you know, Mario, they just sort of, they just happen in front of you.

00:11:44.240 --> 00:11:52.020
And whereas in this game, it's kind of like a mix between the two, like they're, they're just sort of there until you get close to them.

00:11:52.120 --> 00:11:53.280
And then they actually go after you.

00:11:53.420 --> 00:12:01.340
So you, you, you sort of want to just avoid them because you don't want them to seek you out, which is what they will do.

00:12:02.120 --> 00:12:12.160
And then, yeah, there's that whole thing about just not knowing what is traversable or not, because like it ends up feeling that moments that you're just walking through walls.

00:12:12.160 --> 00:12:15.980
And you have to be really, it's kind of tricky.

00:12:16.180 --> 00:12:25.680
You have to really pay attention to like when you're going to be holding down when you jump so you can get, because otherwise you're just going to get stuck near the top of the screen sometimes.

00:12:26.260 --> 00:12:37.080
And so, yeah, a lot of this traversal is, it feels off, but that, that's sort of, that's sort of where I was feeling.

00:12:37.080 --> 00:12:54.220
The loop is you pick from a world on the, on your ship, you grab the coordinates or parts or what have you, go reactivate that world's CompuMind and then go back and to the ship and go to the next place.

